WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.150
Why are the Southern Alps so high?

2
00:00:01.150 --> 00:00:05.050
It comes down to two plates meeting under the South Island.

3
00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:08.650
Aotearoa sits across two plates: the Australian Plate and the Pacific Plate.

4
00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:12.250
In the South Island the boundary between them is the Alpine Fault.

5
00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:15.850
The Pacific Plate pushes into the Australian Plate and slides past it.

6
00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:19.450
Squeezed at the boundary, the crust is forced upwards. That is uplift.

7
00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:22.450
The range rises about 10 mm a year.

8
00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:25.450
Rain, ice and rivers wear it down almost as fast.

9
00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:29.050
Uplift keeps winning, so the Alps stay high. Aoraki is 3,724 m.

10
00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:33.880
Name the plates, then the process, then the result. In that order.

11
00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:39.150
